{"name":"White Elephant","hex":"#dedee5","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"white elephant","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A possession that is unwanted or is a financial burden; an unprofitable investment.","Ellipsis of white elephant exchange."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/white_elephant"}],"entries":[{"title":"White elephant","blocks":[{"text":"A white elephant is a possession that its owner cannot dispose of without extreme difficulty, and whose cost, particularly that of maintenance, is out of proportion to its usefulness. In modern usage, it is a metaphor used to describe an object, construction project, scheme, business venture, facility, etc., considered to lack utility or value relative to its high capital (acquisition) and/or operational (maintenance) costs."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d1/Lord_White_Elephant.jpg/330px-Lord_White_Elephant.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White_elephant","wikidata":"Q877152"},{"title":"White elephant (animal)","blocks":[{"text":"A white elephant is a rare kind of elephant, but not a distinct species. Although often depicted as snow white, their skin is typically a soft reddish-brown, turning a light pink when wet. They have fair eyelashes and toenails. The traditional \"white elephant\" is commonly misunderstood as being albino, but the Thai term, chang samkhan, translates as 'auspicious elephant', being \"white\" in terms of an aspect of purity."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Myanma_Sin_Phyu.jpg/330px-Myanma_Sin_Phyu.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White_elephant_(animal)","wikidata":"Q3629117"},{"title":"Elephant","blocks":[{"text":"Elephants are the largest living land animals. Three living species are currently recognised: the African bush elephant, the African forest elephant, and the Asian elephant. They are the only surviving members of the family Elephantidae and the order Proboscidea; extinct relatives include mammoths and mastodons. Distinctive features of elephants include a long proboscis called a trunk, tusks, large ear flaps, pillar-like legs, and tough but sensitive grey skin. The trunk is prehensile, bringing food and water to the mouth and grasping objects. Tusks, which are derived from the incisor teeth, serve both as weapons and as tools for moving objects and digging. The large ear flaps assist in maintaining a constant body temperature as well as in communication. African elephants have larger ears and concave backs, whereas Asian elephants have smaller ears and convex or level backs."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/African_Bush_Elephant.jpg/330px-African_Bush_Elephant.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elephant","wikidata":"Q7378"},{"title":"White","blocks":[{"text":"White is the lightest color and is achromatic. It is the color of objects such as snow, chalk, and milk, and is the opposite of black. White objects fully reflect and scatter all the visible wavelengths of light. White on television and computer screens is created by a mixture of red, blue, and green light. The color white can be given with white pigments, especially titanium dioxide."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg/330px-Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White","wikidata":"Q23444"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/The_White_Elephant%2C_Punch_103.png/500px-The_White_Elephant%2C_Punch_103.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/The_White_Elephant%2C_Punch_103.png/1280px-The_White_Elephant%2C_Punch_103.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/The_White_Elephant%2C_Punch_103.png/500px-The_White_Elephant%2C_Punch_103.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The British East Africa Company came to regard Uganda as a white elephant when internal conflict made administration of the territory impossible.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AThe_White_Elephant%2C_Punch_103.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5f/Greystaneswaterbridge.jpg/500px-Greystaneswaterbridge.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5f/Greystaneswaterbridge.jpg/1280px-Greystaneswaterbridge.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5f/Greystaneswaterbridge.jpg/500px-Greystaneswaterbridge.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Boothtown Aqueduct, Western Sydney, New South Wales, Australia","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGreystaneswaterbridge.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/RoyalWhiteElephant.jpg/500px-RoyalWhiteElephant.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/RoyalWhiteElephant.jpg/500px-RoyalWhiteElephant.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A royal white elephant, as depicted in a Thai painting","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARoyalWhiteElephant.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4e/Indradeva.jpg/500px-Indradeva.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_cam